e traditional eastern european uh  countries are largely supporting this  but the larger member states are very  concerned and even if they gave it  something a little more realistic like a  fast track to candidate status that  takes a long time to negotiate and i  think there are deep fears about the  size of ukraine and plus you have to  remember there would be freedom of  movement and in a postcove  economic landscape this idea of labor's  jobs uh  free movement of labor and job issues a  lot of member states in the west are a  bit concerned about that yeah i mean you  know you illustrate very well the  dividing lines there  i'm wondering then  what do you think is motivating zelinski  then to push for something that is  practically  practically impossible i mean obviously  the man fears for his life  um there's an existential threat to his  government into his country is this just  a voice of of sheer desperation that is  articulating this request  i mean i think  he he's looking ahead to the future  right so he's trying to maybe use this  opportunity to fast-track ukraine  because there's a deep outpouring of  sympathy and empathy for ukraine and i  think that he's trying to tap into that  so i think  good for him  try your best and i think that as we saw  today in the parliament there were many  many people wearing ukraine shirts on  t-shirts over their suits uh with the  ukraine flag and eu written on it so i  think that there's a great deal of  sympathy for him but it takes a lot for  even i mean there are five uh countries  already on the list and they've been  added for years it takes a long time to  get the laws to match good governance  concerns about um corruption so it's not  something that can be fast-tracked at  all  yeah i mean good governance is still a a  problematic  and thorny issue  for ukraine  let's just imagine though that  there were to be some type of fast track  lane that were to be open to ukraine  how would you expect russia to react i 